          explanation: 'The Ravana Samhita\'s statement on the status of jyotisha: it is not mere superstition but a vedāṅga — a limb of the Vedas, an auxiliary science essential for understanding the Vedic ritual and calendar. "Kāla-vidyā" — the science of time. The verse then gives the reason for studying it: "kālo hi duratikramaḥ" — time is truly insurmountable. You cannot argue with time, negotiate with time, or escape time. The only wise response is to understand it and align with it.',
          science: 'Time perception and decision timing research (Daniel Kahneman on peak-end rule; Philip Zimbardo on time perspective): how humans perceive and relate to time is one of the most powerful predictors of life outcomes. People with "balanced time perspective" (able to draw on past, present, and future orientations appropriately) show greater wellbeing, better decision-making, and more successful relationships. The Ravana Samhita\'s "kāla-jña" — knower of time — is the ancient equivalent of what psychology calls balanced time perspective.',
          lifeLesson: 'The Ravana Samhita invites you to become a "kāla-jña" — a knower of time. This does not mean astrology in the modern sense. It means understanding the temporal structure of your own life: what season are you in? What is ripe now? What needs more time? What has passed its moment? These are not astrological questions; they are questions of self-knowledge in time.',
          keywords: ['JyotishaAsVedanga', 'KalaVidya', 'TimePerspective', 'BalancedTime', 'KalaJna'],
