          explanation: 'The Ravana Samhita\'s Samudrika teaching on the specific sites of bodily reading: hasta-rekha (palmistry — the lines of the hand), lalāṭa (the forehead and its lines), netra (the eyes — their colour, shape, lustre, and movement), and pāda-tala (the soles of the feet). Each is a text in a specific register: the hands reflect action and karma (what has been done and what will be done); the forehead reflects thought and destiny (the lines of accumulated mental patterns); the eyes reflect the state of consciousness itself; the feet reflect the body\'s relationship to the earth and to movement. The Ravana Samhita does not present these as infallible mechanical predictors but as probabilistic readings of accumulated tendencies — the body\'s honest record of where a person has been and where their momentum is carrying them.',
          science: 'Research on facial feature reading, micro-expressions, and embodied personality (Ekman\'s FACS; Physiognomy research): meta-analyses on "thin-slice" judgments confirm that brief exposure to a person\'s face, gait, and bodily presentation allows naive observers to make significantly above-chance assessments of personality traits, health status, and emotional state. The body continuously broadcasts information about inner states through micro-expressions, posture, gait, skin tone, and eye quality — most of which is processed pre-consciously by observers. Samudrika is the explicit systematisation of what human nervous systems do implicitly and rapidly.',
          lifeLesson: 'The Ravana Samhita\'s samudrika teaching asks: do you know how to read the texts that are most available to you? Not palm lines as fatalistic prediction — but the living texts of the people around you. When someone\'s eyes lose their lustre, when their gait becomes heavy, when their forehead furrows in a particular way — what are they telling you that their words are not? And what is your own body broadcasting right now — in your posture, your tension, your breathing pattern — that you are not consciously acknowledging?',
