          explanation: 'The Ravana Samhita lists the ashtanga in exact order of interdependence. Yama is first because it is the foundation on which all others rest. Without yama, pranayama becomes merely breath technique; asana becomes flexibility performance; samadhi becomes structurally impossible. Ravana reversed this: mastered the upper six limbs while violating the first. The Samhita uses his own system to diagnose his failure: a yoga practice that skips the ethical foundation is a structure built from the roof down. The list is not decorative — it is architectural.',
          science: 'Meditation and ethical behaviour research (Condon et al.): regular meditation practice produces measurable increases in prosocial behaviour and reduced implicit bias. However, research also identifies "spiritual bypassing" (Welwood): using contemplative practices to avoid rather than engage with ethical realities. The Ravana Samhita\'s warning about skipping yama maps precisely onto this phenomenon — extraordinary inner development used to justify exemption from ordinary ethical obligation.',
