          transliteration: 'recakaṃ pūrakaṃ caiva kumbhakaṃ trividhaṃ smṛtam | prāṇāyāmena dehasya rogāḥ sarve praṇaśyati ||',
          translation: 'Rechaka, puraka, and kumbhaka — the three types are known. Through pranayama, all diseases of the body are destroyed.',
          hindi: 'रेचक, पूरक और कुम्भक — ये तीन प्रकार स्मृत हैं। प्राणायाम से शरीर के सभी रोग नष्ट होते हैं।',
          explanation: 'The three components of pranayama as a complete system. Puraka (inhalation) — receiving, opening, expansion. Rechaka (exhalation) — releasing what is metabolically finished; the breath-analogue of surrender. Kumbhaka (retention) — suspension at full or empty; concentrated prana held and directed by awareness. "Sarve rogāḥ praṇaśyati" — all diseases are destroyed. In the Ravana Samhita\'s full context, the "disease" most in need of treatment is ahankara — the imbalance that destroyed Ravana himself. He mastered kumbhaka; he could not hold the pause long enough to hear what the silence inside it was saying.',
          science: 'Pranayama and autonomic regulation (Zaccaro et al. systematic review; Brown and Gerbarg): extended exhalation (rechaka-dominant) activates the vagal brake, rapidly reducing heart rate and cortisol within minutes. Kumbhaka at full inhalation increases intrathoracic pressure and produces a strong vagal rebound on release. These are reproducible, measurable autonomic effects requiring no belief system. The Ravana Samhita\'s "all diseases destroyed" is most accurately understood as: all autonomic dysregulation systematically addressed.',
