          transliteration: 'jānāmi dharmaṃ na ca me pravṛttiḥ jānāmyadharmaṃ na ca me nivṛttiḥ | kenāpi devena hṛdisthitena yathā niyukto\'smi tathā karomi ||',
          translation: 'I know dharma, yet I am not inclined toward it. I know adharma, yet I cannot turn away from it. By some deity dwelling in my heart — as I am impelled, so I act.',
          hindi: 'मैं धर्म जानता हूँ, फिर भी उसमें प्रवृत्ति नहीं। अधर्म जानता हूँ, फिर भी निवृत्ति नहीं। हृदय में स्थित किसी देव द्वारा — जैसे नियुक्त हूँ वैसा करता हूँ।',
          explanation: 'The most shattering verse in the Ravana Samhita — placed in Ravana\'s own mouth, and considered by commentators to be his most honest self-disclosure. "Jānāmi dharmaṃ na ca me pravṛttiḥ" — I know dharma, yet I am not inclined toward it. This is not ignorance; it is akrasia — the ancient Greek philosophical problem of acting against one\'s own better judgment. Ravana is not confused about what is right. He knows with complete clarity. He acts against that clarity anyway. The final line — "by some deity in my heart, as I am impelled, so I act" — is simultaneously a confession and a question. Is it genuine surrender to divine will? Or is it the ultimate rationalisation of ego — using the language of divine compulsion to abdicate personal responsibility? The Ravana Samhita leaves this deliberately unresolved. The reader must answer it for themselves.',
          science: 'Research on the intention-behaviour gap and akrasia (Baumeister on self-regulation failure; Metcalfe and Mischel hot-cool system): knowing what is right and acting on it are governed by different neural systems. The "cool" system (prefrontal cortex) generates knowledge of what is right; the "hot" system (limbic, reward circuitry) generates the motivational impulse. When the hot system is sufficiently activated — by desire, threat, habit — it overrides the cool system\'s knowledge. Baumeister\'s research: self-regulation failure is not a failure of knowing but a failure of the motivational system to act on what is known. Ravana\'s "jānāmi dharmaṃ na ca me pravṛttiḥ" is the ancient formulation of the intention-behaviour gap.',
          lifeLesson: 'Where in your own life is this verse true? "I know what is right — and I am not inclined toward it. I know what is harmful — and I cannot stop." This is not a confession of evil; it is the most honest statement available about the human condition. Ravana said it. The Ravana Samhita preserves it as wisdom, not as indictment. The question it asks: what is the "deity in your heart" that impels you against your own knowledge? Naming it honestly — the craving, the fear, the habit, the wound — is the beginning of the only freedom available: not perfect virtue, but honest awareness of the gap between knowing and doing.',
          keywords: ['Akrasia', 'IntentionBehaviourGap', 'KnowingAndDoing', 'RavanasConfession', 'HotCoolSystem'],
