          explanation: 'The Ravana Samhita\'s final, most radical teaching — and its most personal one. "Na janmāntara-sādhyaḥ" — not achievable through many births. "Na tapasā" — not through austerity. Ravana performed austerities that no human in the tradition can match: a thousand years of tapas, cutting off his own heads. "Na yogena" — not through yoga. He mastered seven of the eight limbs. None of it produced liberation. "Sva-svarūpa-anusandhānāt sadyo muktiḥ" — from inquiry into one\'s own essential nature, liberation arises immediately. Not eventually. Not after more practice. Now. The Ravana Samhita\'s closing teaching is the most direct in the entire tradition: all the extraordinary practice is not the cause of liberation. Inquiry into what you actually are — right now, with no preparation, no precondition — is the only cause. Ravana had everything except the willingness to look at what was looking.',
          science: 'Research on self-inquiry and default mode network deactivation (Farb et al.; Brewer; Judson): neuroimaging research distinguishes between two modes of self-referential processing — narrative self-focus (thinking about oneself, past, future — associated with default mode network activation and with depression, anxiety, and rumination) and present-moment experiential awareness (direct experience of the present moment — associated with default mode deactivation and with wellbeing, clarity, and reduced suffering). "Sva-svarūpa-anusandhāna" — inquiry into essential nature — is precisely the turn from narrative self-focus to direct experiential awareness. Research confirms: this shift can occur immediately, requires no preparation, and produces measurable reductions in suffering within minutes.',
          lifeLesson: 'The Ravana Samhita\'s final instruction: "sadyo muktiḥ" — liberation now. Not after more practice, more purification, more qualification. The inquiry is: what is aware right now? Not what you are thinking, feeling, or experiencing — but what is aware of those things? That awareness has no quality of suffering. It is not excited or depressed, not successful or failed, not young or old. It is simply present, clear, and aware. Ravana described this with perfect precision. He did not turn toward it because his extraordinary qualities were too compelling. You have fewer extraordinary qualities to be distracted by. That is your advantage. Use it.',
